      <h2 style="color: #ffffff; font-size: 1.7rem; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.35; margin-bottom: 18px;">Transit Specifications: Specialized Container Trucks for the 850 km Highway Corridor</h2>
      <div style="font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.8; color: #cbd5e1;">**Transit Specifications – Campus‑to‑Home Packers & Movers**

The fleet is built around three MX‑container truck classes, each engineered for rapid, secure relocation of university assets and personal belongings. All units comply with DOT‑type III safety standards, feature reinforced steel frames, and are equipped with a unified telematics suite for real‑time monitoring.

| Model | Overall Length | Container Internal Volume | Payload Capacity |
|-------|----------------|---------------------------|------------------|
| MX‑18 | 18 ft (5.49 m) | 1,200 ft³ (34 m³) | 3,200 lb (1,450 kg) |
| MX‑22 | 22 ft (6.71 m) | 1,650 ft³ (47 m³) | 4,500 lb (2,040 kg) |
| MX‑32 | 32 ft (9.75 m) | 2,400 ft³ (68 m³) | 7,200 lb (3,270 kg) |

**Hydraulic Ramp System**  
Each truck incorporates a dual‑actuator, low‑profile hydraulic ramp (max 2.5 ft rise) that deploys in under 8 seconds. The ramps are rated for 5,500 lb (2,500 kg) and feature anti‑slip surface panels with integrated LED edge lighting for night‑time loading. Sensors monitor ramp position and load weight, feeding data to the central control unit to prevent over‑extension.

**Live GPS Tracking & Telematics**  
A 4G/LTE‑enabled GPS module delivers sub‑meter accuracy, relayed to the cloud platform at 5‑second intervals. The telematics package logs speed, idle time, fuel consumption, and door‑open events. Customers access a secure web portal where they can view a live map, receive ETA updates, and download a tamper‑evident transit log for compliance audits.

**National Highway Route – “Campus‑to‑Home Corridor”**  
The primary corridor leverages the I‑95/US‑1 corridor (Northeast) and I‑40/US‑70 corridor (Midwest‑South) to connect major university campuses with residential hubs. Key waypoints include:

1. **Campus Exit Node** – Dedicated staging lot with 24‑hour security and loading docks.
2. **Interstate Access** – Direct ingress to I‑95 (East Coast) or I‑40 (Central) via grade‑separated ramps, minimizing local traffic interaction.
3. **Mid‑Route Service Centers** – Strategically placed every 200 mi for driver rest, vehicle inspection, and container cleaning.
4. **Home‑Delivery Hub** – Final distribution center equipped with dock‑levelers compatible with the MX‑series ramps.

The route is pre‑programmed into the on‑board navigation system, which dynamically reroutes around congestion, construction, or weather‑related incidents while preserving the optimal fuel‑efficiency profile (average 12 mpg for MX‑32, 15 mpg for MX‑18). This integrated approach ensures that campus‑to‑home moves are completed on schedule, with full visibility and minimal handling risk.</div>
